WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:04.373
On April 20th, oil markets

00:00:04.373 --> 00:00:05.998
did something they never had before

00:00:05.998 --> 00:00:09.540
and they crashed, went
into negative territory

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:11.748
and closed the day at -$40.

00:00:11.748 --> 00:00:16.581
Meanwhile, in a place
called Theydon Bois in Essex

00:00:16.581 --> 00:00:20.540
a group of nine traders
lead by a guy called Cuddles

00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:24.540
made $660 million, over the
course of a couple of hours.

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:27.456
The price of oil has
collapsed to a record low.

00:00:27.456 --> 00:00:29.915
We've never really seen anything like it.

00:00:29.915 --> 00:00:32.790
WTI this morning, down more than 35%.

00:00:32.790 --> 00:00:34.790
People maybe wouldn't
have been so surprised

00:00:34.790 --> 00:00:36.790
if the oil price had fallen to zero.

00:00:36.790 --> 00:00:39.248
But the oil price went to -$38.

00:00:39.248 --> 00:00:41.540
It went far lower than
anyone had expected.

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:43.248
And so lots of people in
the market were saying

00:00:43.248 --> 00:00:45.623
well, hang on, something
else is going on here.

00:00:47.498 --> 00:00:49.956
A couple of months later,
I started to get wind

00:00:49.956 --> 00:00:51.706
through a kind of network of sources

00:00:51.706 --> 00:00:56.040
that a tiny firm in
the outskirts of London

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:59.373
had made a huge amount of money that day

00:00:59.373 --> 00:01:02.040
and potentially had some part
to play in what happened.

00:01:03.331 --> 00:01:05.456
The information we got
was that nine traders

00:01:05.456 --> 00:01:07.165
at Vega Capital London had made in the

00:01:07.165 --> 00:01:10.123
region of $660 million in one day.

00:01:10.123 --> 00:01:12.790
As a group, I think they
made as much money in one day

00:01:12.790 --> 00:01:15.665
as Apple makes from its
international sales in one day.

00:01:15.665 --> 00:01:17.415
I mean, it's an insane sum of money.

00:01:17.415 --> 00:01:19.248
Did they pull off a fantastic trade?

00:01:19.248 --> 00:01:20.415
Did they predict the way the market

00:01:20.415 --> 00:01:22.373
was moving and get it absolutely right?

00:01:22.373 --> 00:01:24.331
Or was there something else
going on and had they actually

00:01:24.331 --> 00:01:28.331
done something that breached market rules

00:01:28.331 --> 00:01:30.540
in a bid to sort of push the market?

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:32.373
And that investigation is ongoing.

00:01:47.706 --> 00:01:50.290
So Paul Commins is a trader

00:01:50.290 --> 00:01:54.081
who cut his teeth in the
pits in the 80s and 90s

00:01:54.081 --> 00:01:57.540
when everyone had a
nickname, his was Cuddles.

00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:01.456
If you can imagine,
that was a very sort of

00:02:01.456 --> 00:02:04.456
cut-throat type of world
where people are doing

00:02:04.456 --> 00:02:06.248
you know, hundreds of thousands

00:02:06.248 --> 00:02:10.206
and millions of dollar trades
by shouting at each other.

00:02:10.206 --> 00:02:11.790
Get five and a half!

00:02:11.790 --> 00:02:13.081
Giving hand signals and then sort

00:02:13.081 --> 00:02:15.040
of scrawling on scraps of paper.

00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:18.665
The culture of the trading pits in London

00:02:18.665 --> 00:02:20.040
was very influenced

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:24.123
by the working class guys
who became traders there.

00:02:24.123 --> 00:02:25.748
They weren't like the city bankers

00:02:25.748 --> 00:02:27.873
that had gone before
them, they weren't usually

00:02:27.873 --> 00:02:30.248
Oxford or Cambridge, you
know, they didn't wear

00:02:30.248 --> 00:02:32.956
Savile Row suits.
They were ordinary working guys

00:02:32.956 --> 00:02:34.956
who happened to have a talent for trading

00:02:34.956 --> 00:02:37.248
and a lot of them came from Essex.

00:02:39.081 --> 00:02:41.081
I think it was 400 traders in the pits

00:02:41.081 --> 00:02:43.248
and Cuddles, in his you
know, oil and gas pit

00:02:43.248 --> 00:02:46.081
was described to us as
amongst the top three

00:02:46.081 --> 00:02:48.498
you know, in amongst that,
was very successful

00:02:48.498 --> 00:02:50.706
and made you know, very
large amounts of money

00:02:50.706 --> 00:02:54.498
as a young man, but
inevitably, mechanization

00:02:54.498 --> 00:02:56.998
and the arrival of electronic markets

00:02:56.998 --> 00:02:59.081
was the kind of death knell for the pits

00:02:59.081 --> 00:03:02.290
and in 2005, the IPE closed down.

00:03:02.290 --> 00:03:04.290
And when the pits shut down,

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:06.248
a lot of the traders who worked there

00:03:06.248 --> 00:03:08.540
lost their jobs or quit or went to work

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:11.581
for financial institutions,
but Paul Commins

00:03:11.581 --> 00:03:15.040
decided to start his own
trading collective out in Essex.

00:03:18.415 --> 00:03:20.165
Some of them are ex-pit guys

00:03:20.165 --> 00:03:21.790
cut from the same cloth as him,

00:03:21.790 --> 00:03:24.790
lots of them are actually
sort of, in their twenties

00:03:24.790 --> 00:03:28.540
and they're, his, you
know, drinking buddies son.

00:03:28.540 --> 00:03:31.665
Or they're his own kid's
friends from football.

00:03:31.665 --> 00:03:34.206
You know, they're young
lads, hungry and ambitious

00:03:34.206 --> 00:03:35.748
and he brings them into the fold.

00:03:35.748 --> 00:03:38.165
And fairly shortly, he's got you know,

00:03:38.165 --> 00:03:40.790
maybe a dozen or so traders that,

00:03:40.790 --> 00:03:42.373
they're independent, let's be clear

00:03:42.373 --> 00:03:43.956
but they're all part of this collective.

00:03:53.915 --> 00:03:56.456
Yeah, I'll let Liam deal with Essex.

00:03:56.456 --> 00:03:58.206
I am from Essex so I feel like I'm free

00:03:58.206 --> 00:04:00.998
to, you know, get into this.

00:04:00.998 --> 00:04:02.665
Sweet, as they say.

00:04:02.665 --> 00:04:04.665
If you are to ask someone in Britain

00:04:04.665 --> 00:04:06.998
how they would imagine an Essex person,

00:04:06.998 --> 00:04:10.165
they would say someone who's
maybe slightly flamboyant,

00:04:10.165 --> 00:04:13.665
with a sort of Cockney accent,
Guy Richie style accent.

00:04:14.956 --> 00:04:16.915
You know, not afraid to splash the cash,

00:04:16.915 --> 00:04:18.331
but is probably pretty bright

00:04:18.331 --> 00:04:19.956
and has done well for themselves.

00:04:19.956 --> 00:04:20.915
Don't even make sense, does it?

00:04:20.915 --> 00:04:22.040
But, you know, these guys,

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:26.206
they drove around very nice
Rolls Royces and Bentleys,

00:04:26.206 --> 00:04:29.956
they went to places like
Marbella on holiday.

00:04:31.331 --> 00:04:34.165
Theydon Bois is a very wealthy place.

00:04:34.165 --> 00:04:36.540
You have to have considerable income

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:38.290
to be able to afford to
live in that village.

00:04:38.290 --> 00:04:39.790
It's a really nice place to live

00:04:39.790 --> 00:04:42.248
and it's 20 minutes tube
ride away from the city.

00:04:46.081 --> 00:04:48.373
Earlier in the summer, we have this lead,

00:04:48.373 --> 00:04:50.540
which is that there's this tiny firm

00:04:50.540 --> 00:04:52.623
called Vega Capital London,

00:04:52.623 --> 00:04:54.873
and they were the biggest winners

00:04:54.873 --> 00:04:57.540
from the biggest oil crash in history.

00:04:57.540 --> 00:04:58.915
You know, anyone that
covers market schemes

00:04:58.915 --> 00:05:00.123
is going to be surprised about that.

00:05:00.123 --> 00:05:02.373
You know, you're thinking
your BP's of the world,

00:05:02.373 --> 00:05:04.623
or your Glencore's, but
it's this tiny firm.

00:05:04.623 --> 00:05:06.498
And one of the big challenges that we had

00:05:06.498 --> 00:05:09.040
was actually finding out who
worked with Vega Capital.

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:10.206
It wasn't immediately obvious,

00:05:10.206 --> 00:05:12.331
and at first we had absolutely no idea.

00:05:12.331 --> 00:05:14.081
If you go to the Vega Capital website,

00:05:14.081 --> 00:05:15.790
it says it's under construction.

00:05:15.790 --> 00:05:18.373
If you do basic online
searching, it's very difficult

00:05:18.373 --> 00:05:21.290
to find anyone who's publicly
connected to the company.

00:05:21.290 --> 00:05:23.790
So, inevitably, we were
incredibly curious at this point.

00:05:23.790 --> 00:05:25.165
Like, who the hell are these people?

00:05:25.165 --> 00:05:26.498
The more reporting we did,

00:05:26.498 --> 00:05:29.248
the more it became clear that
this group were connected

00:05:29.248 --> 00:05:30.831
socially as well as professionally.

00:05:30.831 --> 00:05:33.998
They went to weddings together,
they played golf together,

00:05:33.998 --> 00:05:35.623
they would go on holiday together.

00:05:35.623 --> 00:05:37.748
We found out that a number
of them were members

00:05:37.748 --> 00:05:40.415
of the West Ham
Supporter's Club in London.

00:05:40.415 --> 00:05:42.123
A few of them started companies together.

00:05:42.123 --> 00:05:44.915
So you could see all the
connections between them

00:05:44.915 --> 00:05:47.665
and start to see a
distinct group emerging.

00:05:47.665 --> 00:05:49.831
And it really just was this kind of shock,

00:05:49.831 --> 00:05:52.040
that, you know, this wasn't
just like a hedge fund,

00:05:52.040 --> 00:05:53.165
or a firm you've never heard of.

00:05:53.165 --> 00:05:54.748
This was actually a group of buddies,

00:05:54.748 --> 00:05:56.956
who all had the same
experience on this day

00:05:56.956 --> 00:05:58.956
and had all made a huge amount of money.

00:06:02.373 --> 00:06:04.206
So if you're an oil trader,

00:06:04.206 --> 00:06:05.790
there's a number of
different ways you can trade,

00:06:05.790 --> 00:06:09.790
and probably the most popular
is WTI Futures Contract.

00:06:09.790 --> 00:06:12.123
And it's basically a contract that says

00:06:12.123 --> 00:06:15.123
I'm gonna buy a thousand
barrels of oil from you

00:06:15.123 --> 00:06:16.623
at this point in the future,

00:06:16.623 --> 00:06:18.873
or I'm going to sell a
thousand barrels of oil to you

00:06:18.873 --> 00:06:20.581
at this point in the future.

00:06:21.956 --> 00:06:24.165
It's actually just a financial contract

00:06:24.165 --> 00:06:25.790
to gamble on, to predict

00:06:25.790 --> 00:06:27.581
whether oil was gonna go up or down.

00:06:28.706 --> 00:06:31.581
On the 20th of April in Essex,

00:06:31.581 --> 00:06:33.123
there had been signals for a while

00:06:33.123 --> 00:06:34.956
that this was going to
be an exceptional day

00:06:34.956 --> 00:06:35.998
in oil markets.

00:06:35.998 --> 00:06:38.040
And we know that some of them arose early

00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:40.623
and started their trading in
the early hours of the morning

00:06:40.623 --> 00:06:42.540
while it was still dark outside.

00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:44.581
Essentially what they were doing

00:06:44.581 --> 00:06:49.540
is buying contracts that gave
them an obligation to buy oil

00:06:49.540 --> 00:06:53.165
at whatever the price ended up at 2:30pm.

00:06:53.165 --> 00:06:55.040
So they're basically placing a bet

00:06:55.040 --> 00:06:57.498
that oil's going to
fall throughout the day.

00:06:57.498 --> 00:07:00.373
But they're simultaneously
selling lots of oil as well,

00:07:00.373 --> 00:07:02.873
and whether they make a profit,
how much profit they make

00:07:02.873 --> 00:07:05.165
is the difference between
what they buy the oil for

00:07:05.165 --> 00:07:06.665
and what they sell the oil for.

00:07:06.665 --> 00:07:09.748
Now, of course, we now
know throughout April 20th

00:07:09.748 --> 00:07:11.040
the price dropped and dropped.

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:13.290
So, this is going very well for them.

00:07:13.290 --> 00:07:16.998
You know, they are committed
to selling oil at these prices

00:07:16.998 --> 00:07:18.998
and the prices are continuing to fall

00:07:18.998 --> 00:07:20.748
which means they're gonna
be able to offset it

00:07:20.748 --> 00:07:24.290
and buy oil at cheaper,
at the end of the day.

00:07:24.290 --> 00:07:26.831
Now, once it gets to kind of 1:30, 2pm,

00:07:26.831 --> 00:07:29.956
that's when things start
to get really interesting.

00:07:32.456 --> 00:07:36.123
Suddenly there's a kind of
influx of buyers and sellers,

00:07:36.123 --> 00:07:38.540
you know, as desperation
increases in the market

00:07:38.540 --> 00:07:42.581
and trading volumes go
up. Now at 2:08pm exactly,

00:07:42.581 --> 00:07:45.498
something bizarre and
unprecedented happened,

00:07:45.498 --> 00:07:49.373
which is that oil passes
into negative territory.

00:07:49.373 --> 00:07:52.081
And it fell to a record level of -$38.

00:07:52.081 --> 00:07:53.998
We've never seen anything like it, period,

00:07:53.998 --> 00:07:57.790
in terms of the contraction
of the global economy.

00:07:57.790 --> 00:08:00.373
That huge gulf between what
they were buying the oil for

00:08:00.373 --> 00:08:02.165
and what they were selling it for

00:08:02.165 --> 00:08:03.331
enabled them to make more money

00:08:03.331 --> 00:08:04.706
than they ever thought possible.

00:08:04.706 --> 00:08:09.665
So their profit was the
difference between -$37

00:08:09.665 --> 00:08:11.081
and all of these positive numbers

00:08:11.081 --> 00:08:12.915
on every contract they sold.

00:08:12.915 --> 00:08:16.165
The price of crude
briefly hit -$37 a barrel.

00:08:16.165 --> 00:08:18.665
For one group of traders
operating from a small office,

00:08:18.665 --> 00:08:20.956
it was a very, very profitable day.

00:08:20.956 --> 00:08:21.790
It's important to remember

00:08:21.790 --> 00:08:22.998
that this had never happened before,

00:08:22.998 --> 00:08:25.790
in the history of oil trading.
No-one could've predicted it.

00:08:25.790 --> 00:08:26.998
I can't think of another example

00:08:26.998 --> 00:08:30.748
of where you enter a trade and
you get paid on both sides,

00:08:30.748 --> 00:08:33.581
you get paid both to sell and to buy.

00:08:33.581 --> 00:08:35.165
You know, it's unthinkable.

00:08:39.373 --> 00:08:42.206
One thing we know about
the Cuddles trading arcade

00:08:42.206 --> 00:08:45.540
is that, they were very
comfortable taking large risks

00:08:45.540 --> 00:08:49.165
and, you know, you have to be
clear, this was a large risk.

00:08:49.165 --> 00:08:51.040
Yeah, we spoke to oil traders,

00:08:51.040 --> 00:08:53.373
both people that know these
guys and people that don't,

00:08:53.373 --> 00:08:56.165
and they all say that this is,
you know, incredibly risky.

00:08:56.165 --> 00:08:58.373
And that's actually what
stops a lot of oil traders

00:08:58.373 --> 00:08:59.456
doing trading like this,

00:08:59.456 --> 00:09:02.498
they simply aren't willing
to stomach the risk.

00:09:02.498 --> 00:09:04.123
But Cuddles and his friends were.

00:09:04.665 --> 00:09:07.790
So when the kind of final
calculations came down,

00:09:07.790 --> 00:09:11.123
the group of the nine most profitable traders

00:09:11.123 --> 00:09:14.790
made $660 million, or
thereabouts between them.

00:09:14.790 --> 00:09:18.540
I mean if you can imagine,
I think three or four of them made

00:09:18.540 --> 00:09:20.915
in excess of $100 million each.

00:09:20.915 --> 00:09:23.290
One of the astonishing
things about this trade

00:09:23.290 --> 00:09:24.581
is that two of the individuals

00:09:24.581 --> 00:09:27.498
who made more than $100
million in a single day

00:09:27.498 --> 00:09:30.706
were in their 20s. One
of them was 22 years old,

00:09:30.706 --> 00:09:32.248
you know, only a few years earlier

00:09:32.248 --> 00:09:34.040
he'd been posting on his social media

00:09:34.040 --> 00:09:37.373
about doing teenage stuff
with his mates in town,

00:09:37.373 --> 00:09:39.915
and going to see girls and rap music.

00:09:39.915 --> 00:09:42.706
And here he is a few years
later, making $100 million

00:09:42.706 --> 00:09:44.790
in a single day's trading.

00:09:44.790 --> 00:09:47.123
When me and Liam discovered
that, we were just -

00:09:47.123 --> 00:09:48.081
we couldn't believe it,

00:09:48.081 --> 00:09:49.623
we couldn't believe what we were seeing.

00:09:54.248 --> 00:09:56.081
Oil trading is a zero sum game.

00:09:56.081 --> 00:09:58.040
Anytime you're making
money as an oil trader,

00:09:58.040 --> 00:09:59.206
someone else is losing it.

00:09:59.206 --> 00:10:01.206
Amongst the biggest
losers were the investors

00:10:01.206 --> 00:10:04.831
in this Chinese fund,
Crude Oil Treasure Fund.

00:10:04.831 --> 00:10:07.956
There were thousands of them,
and they lost everything.

00:10:07.956 --> 00:10:10.540
Lots of people had put
their savings into oil funds,

00:10:10.540 --> 00:10:12.665
they lost money that day.

00:10:12.665 --> 00:10:14.915
Big banks and brokers who
sort of stand in the middle

00:10:14.915 --> 00:10:17.123
of trading parties, they
lost money that day.

00:10:17.123 --> 00:10:18.290
And another interesting thing

00:10:18.290 --> 00:10:22.540
is that oil producing countries
like Kuwait or Canada,

00:10:22.540 --> 00:10:27.540
they sell oil as an average
of the WTI closing price

00:10:27.623 --> 00:10:29.081
over the preceding month.

00:10:29.081 --> 00:10:31.665
So the fact that one of
those numbers in that average

00:10:31.665 --> 00:10:34.248
was -$37 meant if you can imagine,

00:10:34.248 --> 00:10:36.915
countries like Saudi Arabia and Kuwait

00:10:36.915 --> 00:10:38.498
potentially lost a couple of dollars

00:10:38.498 --> 00:10:41.040
on every single barrel of
oil they sold that month.

00:10:44.206 --> 00:10:45.748
Vega Capital's traders made

00:10:45.748 --> 00:10:47.748
such an extraordinary sum of money,

00:10:47.748 --> 00:10:49.956
that it was inevitable
there would be some scrutiny

00:10:49.956 --> 00:10:51.206
of what they did.

00:10:51.206 --> 00:10:53.165
But it wasn't just the
sums of money they made,

00:10:53.165 --> 00:10:54.331
in the vital half an hour

00:10:54.331 --> 00:10:56.373
before the settlement price was set,

00:10:56.373 --> 00:10:59.790
they were by far the biggest
sellers of oil futures

00:10:59.790 --> 00:11:02.415
in the market, which is an
incredible thing to think about,

00:11:02.415 --> 00:11:04.290
when you consider that
the other participants

00:11:04.290 --> 00:11:06.956
in that oil market are gonna
be the world's biggest banks,

00:11:06.956 --> 00:11:09.456
you know, oil majors, BP and Shell,

00:11:09.456 --> 00:11:11.998
and here you have a group
of nine guys in Essex

00:11:11.998 --> 00:11:13.706
who were having this significant influence

00:11:13.706 --> 00:11:15.873
over a global market for oil.

00:11:15.873 --> 00:11:17.748
I mean, I think you have to assume that

00:11:17.748 --> 00:11:21.290
if these guys had made $7
million or $10 million,

00:11:21.290 --> 00:11:24.081
they probably would be
celebrating in Essex right now,

00:11:24.081 --> 00:11:27.248
but the fact that they
made closer to $700 million

00:11:27.248 --> 00:11:31.623
has meant that they are facing
understandable scrutiny.

00:11:31.623 --> 00:11:35.123
We wrote individually
to all the Vega traders

00:11:35.123 --> 00:11:37.790
who did exceptionally well that day,

00:11:37.790 --> 00:11:40.248
and we got a letter in
response from a law firm

00:11:40.248 --> 00:11:41.956
representing the group,

00:11:41.956 --> 00:11:44.290
and the law firm was at pains to point out

00:11:44.290 --> 00:11:46.998
number one, they haven't been
accused of any wrongdoing,

00:11:46.998 --> 00:11:51.123
that they all traded
independently and separately,

00:11:51.123 --> 00:11:53.498
and they'd basically all
made up their own minds

00:11:53.498 --> 00:11:56.040
that this was what was going
to happen in oil markets

00:11:56.040 --> 00:11:58.206
based on publicly available knowledge,

00:11:58.206 --> 00:12:00.873
and decided to execute this trade.

00:12:02.290 --> 00:12:03.873
What I love about these stories,

00:12:03.873 --> 00:12:06.540
is there's a real variance of opinion

00:12:06.540 --> 00:12:08.248
and some people will look at what happened

00:12:08.248 --> 00:12:09.998
